description:
Mailcheer's public API.

It covers two uses: sending individual (transactional) emails from your application, and driving your lists and campaigns — from code or from an AI agent, through the MCP server.

## Connected in three minutes

1. **Create a key** under Settings → API in your workspace. It starts with `mch_live_` and carries the permissions you choose.
2. **Call `GET /api/v1/me`.** It returns the workspace, the key's permissions, the remaining quota, the **verified** domains and the registered senders. That is what tells you which address to write from: without it, your first send fails on `from`.
3. **Send** with `POST /api/v1/emails`.

```bash
curl https://mailcheer.com/api/v1/emails \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer mch_live_…" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "from": "Your brand <hello@yourdomain.com>",
    "to": "customer@example.com",
    "subject": "Your September invoice",
    "html": "<p>Here it is.</p>"
  }'
```

## What applies to every call

- **Base address** `https://mailcheer.com`; every path starts with `/api/v1`. Bodies and responses in JSON (`Content-Type: application/json`).
- **Authentication** `Authorization: Bearer mch_live_…`, or the `X-Api-Key` header if your gateway swallows the first.
- **Rate limit** 600 requests per minute per key. Beyond that: 429. Every `429` and every `423` carries `Retry-After`, in seconds.
- **Errors** always the same shape — read `error.code` (or `name`), never `message`, which is written for a human and may change.
- **Language** messages follow your `Accept-Language` header: **English by default, French if you ask for it** — and only the first preference is read. The `code` never changes language. A key can also have its own language (Settings → API): it answers when the call asks for none.
- **Quota** every authenticated response — success or error, `402` included — carries `Mailcheer-Quota-Limit`, `Mailcheer-Quota-Used`, `Mailcheer-Quota-Remaining` and `Mailcheer-Quota-Reset`: your software can show “470 emails left until October 1” without an extra call. If the quota cannot be read at that moment, the response goes out without them. A key with a monthly limit (Settings → API) also carries `Mailcheer-Key-Quota-Limit`, `-Used` and `-Remaining`; beyond its limit: `402 key_quota_exceeded`.
- **One workspace per key.** Nothing crosses from one workspace to another.

## Coming from Resend

The fields and the response of `POST /api/v1/emails` are modeled on theirs: `reply_to`, `tags` as a `{name, value}` array, and an error that also carries `statusCode` / `message` / `name`. A migration comes down to changing the base address and the key. One difference to know: the response is **202**, not 200 — the message is accepted, not delivered yet.

## From an AI agent (MCP)

The MCP server is at `https://mailcheer.com/api/mcp` — JSON-RPC over **POST**, Streamable HTTP transport, sessionless: every request carries the key in the same `Authorization` header. It opens no stream (a `GET` answers 405, which clients know how to read). The tools call this same API with your key: same permissions, same quota, same refusals.

## Test mode

A key that starts with `mch_test_` (the **Test key** switch under Settings → API) checks every send exactly like a live one — verified `from`, recipients, suppression list, sending status and daily limit, monthly quota, free-plan domain rule, `Idempotency-Key` — and returns the same response, refusals included. **Nothing goes out and the quota is not touched**: the `Mailcheer-Quota-*` headers show the real figures, unchanged. Every authenticated response carries `Mailcheer-Mode: test` or `live`.

Two seconds later the email gets simulated events and your webhooks really receive them, marked `"test": true` next to `type`, with the `data` of a real event. Any address is delivered; `bounced@simulator.mailcheer.com` bounces, `complained@simulator.mailcheer.com` is delivered then complains (a `+label` may follow the word). With a live key, these addresses are refused.

A test key has one scope, `emails:send`, simulated: it neither reads nor changes any real data (`403 insufficient_scope` elsewhere), and only sees its own test emails. At most 1,000 test addresses per 24 hours per workspace.

## Five rules apply to every send, without exception

1. `from` must be on a domain verified in the workspace;
2. an address on the suppression list is refused, with its reason — and the whole call fails, never a silent partial send. An unsubscribe only refuses sends that carry `unsubscribe_url` (a newsletter, prospecting): an individual email without it still reaches that person. A bounce, a complaint or a manual suppression refuses everything;
3. your plan's monthly quota counts these sends the same as campaigns — and on the free plan, a sending domain's free emails serve **one** workspace a month (`402 free_plan_domain_used` otherwise);
4. a bounce or complaint rate that is too high suspends sending;
5. a subscriber added through the API receives a confirmation, unless `double_opt_in: false` is explicit — and then you bear responsibility for the consent.

## Webhooks — Mailcheer calls you
The API answers when you ask it; a webhook tells you without being asked. Subscribe an address of your own (`POST /api/v1/webhooks`, or Settings → API) and Mailcheer posts JSON to it on every event you choose.

**Verify the signature, always.** Every call carries the header `Mailcheer-Signature: t=<unix timestamp>,v1=<hex>` where `v1` is the HMAC-SHA256 of `"<t>.<raw body>"` signed with the subscription's secret. Compare in constant time, and reject a `t` older than 300 seconds — without that second check, an intercepted call stays replayable forever.

```js
import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from "node:crypto";

// secret: "whsec_…"
// header: the Mailcheer-Signature header
// rawBody: the body exactly as received, untouched
export function verifySignature(
  secret,
  header,
  rawBody,
  nowS = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),
) {
  if (!header) return false;
  const parts = new Map();
  for (const piece of header.split(",")) {
    const i = piece.indexOf("=");
    if (i > 0) {
      parts.set(piece.slice(0, i).trim(), piece.slice(i + 1));
    }
  }
  const t = Number(parts.get("t"));
  const v1 = parts.get("v1");
  if (!Number.isFinite(t) || !v1) return false;
  if (Math.abs(nowS - t) > 300) return false;
  const expected = Buffer.from(
    createHmac("sha256", secret)
      .update(`${t}.${rawBody}`, "utf8")
      .digest("hex"),
    "utf8",
  );
  const received = Buffer.from(v1, "utf8");
  if (expected.length !== received.length) return false;
  return timingSafeEqual(expected, received);
}
```

**Answer 2xx in under fifteen seconds.** Everything else is a failure: Mailcheer tries 5 times in all — the next attempts 30 s, 2 min, 10 min and 1 h after the previous failure — then marks the delivery `failed`, which you can replay (`POST /api/v1/webhooks/{id}/deliveries/{deliveryId}/replay`). Do your work in the background and answer straight away — a long job is indistinguishable from an outage.

**The same event can arrive twice** (a retry after a lost response). `id` is stable from one attempt to the next: keep it and ignore duplicates.

Bodies all have the same shape: `{ "id": "evt_…", "type": "…", "created_at": "…", "data": { … } }`. The events: `email.sent`, `email.failed`, `email.delivered`, `email.opened`, `email.clicked`, `email.bounced`, `email.complained`, `email.received`, `subscriber.created`, `subscriber.unsubscribed` and `quota.threshold_reached` — the `data` of each is described in the `webhooks` section of this file.

**The test event carries none of the fields of the real event.** `POST /api/v1/webhooks/{id}/test` posts, under the subscription's first event `type`, a `data` that holds only `{ "test": true, "message": "…" }`. Check `data.test === true` before reading anything else, and answer 2xx without processing it.

**`quota.threshold_reached`** — the monthly quota has reached 50, 80, 95 or 100%. Sent once a month per threshold, at the moment the email that crosses it is accepted; if one send crosses several thresholds at once, only the highest is sent. The numbers are those of `GET /api/v1/me`, and the counter resets on the 1st at 00:00 UTC:

```json
{
  "id": "evt_…",
  "type": "quota.threshold_reached",
  "created_at": "2026-09-24T16:02:11.000Z",
  "data": {
    "threshold": 80,
    "plan": "free",
    "quota": 3000,
    "sent": 2400,
    "in_flight": 35,
    "remaining": 565,
    "resets_at": "2026-10-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    "period": "2026-09"
  }
}
```

At 50, 80 and 95%, the workspace owner and administrators also receive an email. At 100%, only the webhook fires: from then on, sends are refused with `402 quota_exceeded`.

